Human Rights

Purpose

Our chatbot will help understand their basic Human Rights using simple language. It will answer common questions and direct us. 

1. Right to Freedom

You have the right to freedom. You should not be arrested or locked up without a good reason. EX. If you're arrested, you have the right to remain silent. 

Example Question: I have been pulled over, and the Officer says he is going to search my vehicle. What can I do?

Chatbot Response: You can use your 4th Ammendment right to decline this order. You have the right to refuse the search of a Police Officer.

2. Right to Free Expression

You have the right to hold ideas and have your say - even if other people don't like your views. And you have the right to read books and newspapers, if you want to. 

Example Question: Me and a few others are holding a peaceful protest to fight for the rights of what we believe in. The police want to take down my protest even though I haven't caused any harm. Should I obey this order from the police?

Chatbot Response: You should be able to say and read what you like as long as you respect others. This includes Peaceful Protest. You have the right to freedom of expression. 

3. Right to a Fair Trial

You are innocent until proven guilty. You have the right to defend yourself, and get legal help, and told be told clearly about the case against you.

Example Question: What are my rights if I do not understand the language used in courts?

Chatbot Response: Your Miranda Rights are your rights that can be used to your advantage in courts. Some of these rights include, "You have the right to an Attorney. If you can't afford one, one will be appointed to you", "You may stop answering questions at any time.". If these rights are not read to you, your case will remain as inadmissable.

4. Freedom of Religion

You are free to believe what you like. Other people should not tell you what to believe. 

Example Question: Can my school or workplace stop me from practicing my religion or wearing a religious clothing? 

Chatbot Response: The First Ammendment guarantees freedoms concerning religion. It forbids Congress from promoting one religion over others and also restricting an individuals religious practices. 

